const NES_EMU_ACCEPTANCE_HTML = "<section class=\"gbemu-hero\"><div class=\"gbemu-hero-num\"><b>Acceptance</b><span>build specification</span></div><div class=\"gbemu-hero-txt\"><h3>Acceptance: how to build it, exactly as our core runs it</h3><p>The tests a build must pass, what each section proves, the broken builds that prove the tests can fail, and how to obtain the test corpus. Every statement says how it is known. This is one area of the build specification; the index lists all eight.</p></div></section>\n<nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>In this area</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance\">Acceptance: the tests a build must pass</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">The corpus: where the acceptance suite's inputs come from</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit\">The fix audit: bugs other projects published, checked against this core</a></nav>\n<section class=\"sect nesemu-chapter\" id=\"nesemu-80-acceptance-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"nesemu-80-acceptance\">Acceptance: the tests a build must pass</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance-s1\">1. The sections</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance-s2\">2. The reference's figures</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance-s3\">3. Proof that the suite can fail (auditors)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance-s4\">4. Glossary</a></nav>\n<p>The acceptance suite scores <b>any</b> implementation behind <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a>. Its inputs are described in <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a>; the words in <b>bold capitals</b> below and the terms <em>frame</em>, <em>item</em>, <em>row</em>, <em>reference</em>, <em>draws</em>, <em>loads</em>, <em>floor</em>, <em>subset run</em> and <em>broken build</em> are defined in section 4.</p>\n<p><b>Rules: what is accepted.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>A build is accepted when <code>node test/acceptance/run.mjs --impl &lt;your build&gt;.wasm</code> prints <code>ACCEPTANCE GREEN, 9 of 9 sections passed</code> and exits with code 0.</li><li>The build must be a freestanding <code>wasm32</code> module with no imports, exporting <code>memory</code> and the REQUIRED functions of <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a>.</li><li>The suite drives the module through the site's own host (<code>web/core.js</code>): the same memory allocation, call sequence, container parsing and header correction the play page uses. It never reads <code>src/</code>.</li><li>A <em>subset run</em> is never an acceptance verdict, whatever it prints (\"Running part of the suite\", below).</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Driving the build through the site's own host means a build that passes is a build the site can load. Reading no source means the suite judges behaviour only.</p></div>\n<p><b>What an implementer receives and what it does not.</b> The suite's program files (<code>test/acceptance/</code>) and its inputs (the test cartridges, the cartridge library, the disk images and the drive firmware of <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a>) are not part of this specification. Whoever scores a build runs the suite on it. An implementer without them checks a build against the pass conditions of section 1 and the reading rules of section 1.1 with programs of its own; section 2 gives the figures the reference reaches.</p>\n<p><b>Exit codes</b> (decision table; exactly one applies):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>the run</th><th>exit code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>every section passed</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>at least one section failed, and every section loaded its full input set</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>at least one section could not load its full input set (the section and the missing input are named)</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A run in which a section could not load its full input set never exits 0.</p>\n<p><b>Running part of the suite.</b> <code>--rows</code> and <code>--max</code> make a <em>subset run</em>. <code>--only</code> is not a subset run, but a run with <code>--only</code> cannot print <code>9 of 9 sections passed</code>, so it is not an acceptance verdict either (rule 1).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>option</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>--only A0,A2</code></td><td>runs only the named sections (comma-separated)</td></tr><tr><td><code>--rows &lt;names&gt;</code></td><td>runs only the matching items of each section run. The list is split at <code>|</code> if it contains one, otherwise at commas, so <code>|</code> separates names that themselves contain commas. A token <code>m&lt;N&gt;</code> selects mapper N exactly (<code>m1</code> selects mapper 1, not mappers 10-19 or 100-199); any other name selects every item whose name contains it</td></tr><tr><td><code>--max &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>runs only the first n items (after <code>--rows</code>) of each section run</td></tr><tr><td><code>--deadline &lt;seconds&gt;</code></td><td>the per-item deadline (default 600 s)</td></tr><tr><td><code>--deadline-vs &lt;seconds&gt;</code></td><td>the deadline of the VS play check (default 7,200 s)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A subset run says so on every summary line, for example <code>ACCEPTANCE GREEN, 1 of 1 sections passed (SUBSET, not an acceptance verdict)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Rules: the deadline on every item.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Each <em>item</em> is measured in a worker thread: a board row, a cartridge, a disk, an audio item, a peripheral row, a VS title, a save-state item, and A0's checks as one item.</li><li>If an item has not returned a result 600 s (wall-clock seconds, counted from the item's start; <code>--deadline &lt;seconds&gt;</code>) after it started, the thread is stopped and the item is a FAIL that names it: <code>TIMED OUT: no result within … s</code>. The suite then carries on with the next item.</li><li>The third timed-out item in one section ends that section: its remaining items are not run, and the section is a FAIL.</li><li><code>tools/vs_play.mjs</code> (section A7) runs as a child process with its own deadline, 7,200 s (wall-clock seconds from its start; <code>--deadline-vs</code>).</li><li>Every run ends with one line giving its slowest item, that item's time in seconds, and the deadline, so the margin is a reading.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> A build that never returns from a call gets a red verdict, never a hang. Without the deadline, a build with a sprite-DMA bug that never returns hangs the suite with no verdict. Section 3 shows both outcomes on one broken build.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-80-acceptance-s1\">1. The sections</h4>\n<p>Frame counts in this table and in 1.1 count whole <em>frames</em> run since the cartridge or disk was loaded (power-on); \"at frame 240\" means after the 240th frame has finished.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>section</th><th>what it runs</th><th>passes when</th><th>what it proves</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>A0 ABI</b></td><td>the module itself; Super Mario Bros. twice; two machines in one instance</td><td>no imports; <code>memory</code> and all 77 REQUIRED functions exported (13 of them the persistence exports of <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a> 5.8); <code>nes_abi_version()</code> = 1; two power-ons agree after 120 frames (frame, program counter, cycle count); two machines stepped alternately in ONE instance each equal their solo run; the framebuffer is 256 × 240 words</td><td>the contract holds, and <b>all machine state is in the context</b> (state anywhere else leaks between the two machines)</td></tr><tr><td><b>A1 BOARD</b></td><td>the rows of <code>test/BOARD-ROWS.json</code> marked tier 1 (171 rows): public test programs, each judged by its own verdict</td><td>every row the reference passes passes</td><td>the pinned test cartridges' own verdicts: CPU, picture unit, audio registers, DMA, mapper chips, the two test disks</td></tr><tr><td><b>A2 MAPPERS</b></td><td>one cartridge per mapper number in the recorded set (section 2; mapper 20 is A4's)</td><td>each mounts as that number and <em>draws</em> at frame 240</td><td>every mapper chip boots real firmware</td></tr><tr><td><b>A3 LIBRARY</b></td><td>the pinned library sample</td><td>at least the <em>floor</em> of its cartridges <em>draw</em> at frame 240</td><td>the library as a whole</td></tr><tr><td><b>A4 DISK SYSTEM</b></td><td>the pinned disk sample, through the drive firmware, 900 frames after the first insertion</td><td>at least the floor of disks <em>load</em></td><td>the drive, its interrupts and the transfer model</td></tr><tr><td><b>A5 AUDIO</b></td><td>15 items: the four mixer test programs, three titles, PAL, and every expansion chip</td><td>each item's features against the reference's: SPEC ≥ 0.90, ENV ≥ 0.90, |LEVEL| ≤ 1.0 dB</td><td>the output path to the sample, which no board row hears</td></tr><tr><td><b>A6 PERIPHERALS</b></td><td>19 rows: known titles driven with scripted input</td><td>every PROOF row's predicate TRUE, every CONTROL row's FALSE</td><td>Zapper, Arkanoid (NES and Famicom), Power Pad, Family Trainer, Four Score, Famicom 4-player adapter, microphone</td></tr><tr><td><b>A7 VS</b></td><td>the 20 declared VS titles</td><td>each title's VS mode, its six <code>nes_vs_info</code> fields and its 64-entry colour table equal the reference's; <code>tools/vs_play.mjs</code> exits 0 (every title takes a coin, starts, and plays)</td><td>the VS machine mode</td></tr><tr><td><b>A8 SAVE STATE</b></td><td>6 items: NROM, MMC3, MMC5, VRC6, VRC7, a disk mid-load</td><td>the byte range from the context to the host's last allocation, restored in place and into a fresh machine that loaded the same file, gives identical frames and audio for the next 120 frames; the picture moved</td><td>the save-state contract of <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">1.1 How each section reads a result</h5>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">A1: an ordinary board row</h5>\n<p>The suite runs each row as the host's board runner does, for at most 2,400 frames. At the end of each frame, these checks are made in this order; the first that gives a verdict ends the row:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>The status-byte protocol</b> (checked at the end of every frame). If the bytes at <code>$6001</code>, <code>$6002</code>, <code>$6003</code> read <code>$DE</code>, <code>$B0</code>, <code>$61</code> (the protocol's signature), the status byte is <code>$6000</code>:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>$80</code>: still running; go to the next frame.</li><li><code>$81</code>: the cartridge asks for a reset. Press reset ONCE per request: run 250 ms of CPU time (the CPU clock rate ÷ 4, in CPU cycles) after the frame end at which <code>$81</code> was read, then reset. Do not press again until the status byte has been read with a value other than <code>$81</code>. At most 4 resets per row; a fifth request is read as the result <code>$81</code>.</li><li>any other value: the result. <code>$00</code> passes; any other value fails.</li></ul>\n<p>Checks 2 to 5 are made only at the end of frames whose number is a multiple of 8.</p></li><li><b>The on-screen text</b>: <code>FAILED</code> anywhere on screen fails; otherwise <code>PASSED</code> anywhere on screen passes.</li><li><b>The <code>$HH</code> result code</b>: when the whole screen, blanks removed, is exactly a dollar sign and two hex digits. Code <code>$01</code> passes; any other code fails.</li><li><b>A printed checksum</b> (only rows with <code>expect_crc</code>): when the last hex number on screen has exactly eight digits, it passes if it is one of the row's <code>expect_crc</code> values and fails otherwise.</li><li><b>The mapper suite's own report</b> (when the screen shows <code>HOLY MAPPEREL</code> and <code>DETAILED TEST RESULT:</code>): passes when the detected mapper number equals the one in the cartridge's file name (checked only when the file name starts with <code>M</code> and a number), the detailed result is <code>0000</code>, and no memory region reads <code>PROBLEM</code>; fails otherwise.</li></ol>\n<p>A row that reaches 2,400 frames with no verdict scores no verdict (CANNOT-SCORE).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> One press per request: <code>$81</code> does not clear itself, and the cartridge's own reset handler runs for about two frames before it writes a new status. A runner that treats every sighting of <code>$81</code> as a new request presses again inside that window, and the cartridge counts resets to choose its next test.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">A1: the five rows with their own protocol</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>row</th><th>protocol</th><th>PASS when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>nestest</b> (<code>other/nestest.nes</code>)</td><td>starts at <code>$C000</code>; compared instruction by instruction with the golden log (<a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a> section 1)</td><td>every instruction's PC, A, X, Y, P, S and cycle count equal the golden log, then <code>$02</code> = <code>$03</code> = 0</td></tr><tr><td><b>read_joy3/test_buttons</b></td><td>reads the prompt on screen and holds exactly that button (6 frames held, 6 released)</td><td>the cartridge's own pass; a wrong bit order fails</td></tr><tr><td><b>read_joy3/count_errors</b></td><td>runs 600 frames</td><td>the <code>Conflicts: N/1000</code> line is on screen after 600 frames and no failure text is</td></tr><tr><td><b>the two test disks</b></td><td>booted through the drive firmware, then run for the row's frame budget (1,800 frames unless the row gives one)</td><td>after the frame budget, each sub-test's mark (the first non-blank character of its row on screen: O pass, X fail) is O; at least one mark is present</td></tr><tr><td><b>AccuracyCoin</b> (<code>reader: \"accuracycoin\"</code>)</td><td>the steps below</td><td>the steps below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>AccuracyCoin</b>, step by step (frames counted from power-on, as above):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Run 120 frames. Hold Start from the start of frame 121; release it at the start of frame 131.</li><li>Run, frame by instruction, until 6,000 frames have finished.</li><li>Read twice: after frame 4,500 and after frame 6,000. Each reading takes the cartridge's zero-page counters <code>PostAllPassTally</code>, <code>PostAllTestTally</code>, <code>RunningAllTests</code> and <code>AllTestMenuTotalSkipped</code>, at the addresses its source declares, and the result bytes at <code>$0400-$04FF</code>.</li><li>Count the result bytes the way the cartridge's own results page counts them: <code>$FF</code> skipped, bit 0 set passed; only the scored tests the source declares are counted.</li><li>The verdict, as a decision table:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>condition</th><th>verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the CPU jammed, or the run did not reach frame 4,500</td><td>CANNOT-SCORE</td></tr><tr><td>the two readings differ, or <code>RunningAllTests</code> ≠ 0, or the test tally ≠ the number of scored tests the source declares</td><td>CANNOT-SCORE</td></tr><tr><td>otherwise, pass tally = test tally = the declared count, none skipped, and the result bytes agree with the tally</td><td>PASS</td></tr><tr><td>otherwise</td><td>FAIL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>On the pinned copy (<a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a> section 1, commit <code>673ef550db296136d52229961e7d39366116882a</code>) the source declares <b>144</b> scored tests, so PASS reads \"Tests passed: 144 / 144\".</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Both counts are needed. Taken alone, the tally of a run that has not started reads 0 of 0 and passes; taken alone, the result bytes of a run that stopped half-way read as a list of failures. The 5 Power On State tests store their result at <code>$03FF</code>, draw power-on contents and judge nothing; the cartridge's own tally skips them and so does this reader.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">A2 and A3</h5>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Power the cartridge on through the host, header correction on.</li><li>Run 240 frames.</li><li>Read the mapper number the core reports and the number of distinct palette indices on screen.</li><li>The cartridge <em>draws</em> when the CPU is not jammed, 240 frames ran, and two or more palette indices are on screen.</li></ol>\n<p>Each also prints, <b>as a reading and not a requirement</b>, how many frame-240 pictures are bit-identical to the reference's.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">A4</h5>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Load the drive firmware and the disk through the host; eject at power-on.</li><li>Wait 8 frames; insert side 0 (the host's own drive helper).</li><li>Run 900 frames from the insertion.</li><li>Sample the program counter at the end of each of the last 60 of those frames.</li><li>The disk <em>loads</em> when the CPU is not jammed, two or more palette indices are on screen at the end, AND at least one sample is below <code>$E000</code>: the disk's own program is running from the adapter's RAM, not the drive firmware's error screen.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> \"Draws\" alone is not enough: the firmware's error screens draw too, so the <code>fds-byte-too-fast</code> broken build (section 3) passes a draws-only test.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">A5</h5>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Render each item's audio at the CPU clock rate ÷ 40 samples per second.</li><li>Cut it into windows of 1/15 s (4,096 samples each, Hann window).</li><li>Sum each window's energy into half-octave bands from 50 Hz to 16 kHz.</li><li>Align with the reference's grid by a lag search of ±6 windows.</li><li>Compute: <b>SPEC</b>, the Pearson correlation of the two band grids over the windows where either side is above −70 dB; <b>ENV</b>, the correlation of the two loudness curves (one value per window); <b>LEVEL</b>, the overall energy ratio in dB.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Tolerant on purpose: a correct build may start a note a frame apart. The thresholds are <b>tuned</b> (chosen because they work, not derived); the reference scores 1.000 on itself.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">A6</h5>\n<p>Each row is one of the PROOF and CONTROL rows of the native peripherals gate. It applies its script at the start of the named frames (pad, Zapper aim and trigger, Vaus knob and fire, mat buttons, microphone, expansion device, port device) and reads what the TITLE did, a RAM byte or a sprite's X, never the core's own port.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">A8</h5>\n<p>Snapshot after a fixed number of frames; then press buttons through the 120 frames that follow, so that the game state moves.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-80-acceptance-s2\">2. The reference's figures</h4>\n<p><code>test/acceptance/record.mjs</code> records these from the reference build into <code>test/acceptance/expected.json</code>, which names the recorded build by SHA-256: <code>11bdfbfa…</code>, 308,469 bytes, the shipped core. A build is compared with that recording, not with a fresh run of the core.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>section</th><th>the reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A0</td><td>8 of 8 checks</td></tr><tr><td>A1</td><td>every tier 1 row passes but one, and that one is not required of a build: <code>other/test28.nes</code> scores no verdict, because the host's screen reader does not know that cartridge's 8×16 font. <code>AccuracyCoin.nes</code> is recorded as <b>PASS, 144 of 144</b> on the pinned copy (<code>673ef550db296136d52229961e7d39366116882a</code>), read by the suite's own A1 reader. The public figure, <b>147 of 147</b>, is AccuracyCoin's own tally on its 4 November 2026 build, 66e8711 (147 scored tests); the suite does not run that build</td></tr><tr><td>A2</td><td><b>202 of 202</b> mapper numbers in the recorded set have a cartridge in the corpus that the reference mounts as that number and draws. <b>101 have none</b> (named on every run, not scored): 6, 17, 31, 35, 36, 40, 43, 46, 60, 63, 77, 81, 101, 106, 107, 125, 127, 146, 151, 164, 166, 170, 175, 182, 187, 190, 193, 198, 199, 200, 203, 205, 208, 219, 224, 227, 229, 230, 244, 250, 254, 256, 258, 263, 264, 266, 284, 289, 290, 292, 299, 302, 303, 305, 306, 307, 308, 309, 312, 313, 319, 326, 330, 334, 346, 347, 351, 368, 374, 380, 394, 396, 415, 417, 418, 422, 513, 516, 517, 518, 519, 522, 525, 526, 533, 534, 535, 538, 539, 540, 544, 548, 549, 553, 554, 557, 563, 564, 576, 585, 594: boards present only as headers the host corrects to another number, as hacks, or not in the library at all. Mapper 20 is A4's. The recorded set is every one of the 304 mapper numbers this specification documents</td></tr><tr><td>A3</td><td>draws <b>196 of 208</b> (every 10th of 2,073 verified dumps); floor <b>193</b></td></tr><tr><td>A4</td><td><b>50 of 73</b> disks load (every 9th of 649); floor <b>48</b>. The others are titles whose frame-end samples all fall in drive-firmware routines, or that wait in the firmware's own screens</td></tr><tr><td>A5</td><td>15 of 15 items (identical to itself)</td></tr><tr><td>A6</td><td>19 of 19 rows at their expectation</td></tr><tr><td>A7</td><td>20 of 20 titles' colour tables; <code>vs_play</code> exit 0</td></tr><tr><td>A8</td><td>6 of 6 items identical in place and in a fresh machine</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rules: the floors.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The floor of A3 is 98% of the reference's draw count, rounded up: 196 × 0.98 = 192.08, floor <b>193</b>. The floor of A4 is 95%, rounded up: 50 × 0.95 = 47.5, floor <b>48</b>.</li><li>A build passes A3 when at least 193 of the 208 cartridges draw, and A4 when at least 48 of the 73 disks load.</li><li>A2 has no floor: every mapper number in the recorded set must mount as itself and draw.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The floors are a policy, <b>tuned</b> rather than measured: a correct build that differs on a marginal title by a frame of timing should not fail the library; a build that loses a family of titles must.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-80-acceptance-s3\">3. Proof that the suite can fail (auditors)</h4>\n<p><code>node test/acceptance/prove_can_fail.mjs</code> builds <b>broken copies of the reference core</b> and runs each through the sections it must break. Building a broken core needs <code>src/</code>, so this check is run on the reference core, not on yours. Its steps:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Copy <code>src/</code> to a temporary directory and build it UNPATCHED with <code>tools/build.sh</code>'s own flags. It must be byte-identical to <code>build/nes.wasm</code>; otherwise the check stops (a red could be the flags).</li><li>Run the unbroken reference on every broken build's subset of sections and items. It must be green on each; otherwise the check stops (a red could be the subset).</li><li>For each broken build in the table: apply ONE fault to a fresh copy, compile with the same flags, run the sections in its \"must red\" column. Each must be red.</li><li>A broken build may also declare lines its output must contain (<code>EXPECT_TEXT</code>), so that it is red for the declared reason.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>broken build</th><th>the fault</th><th>must red</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>abi-missing</code></td><td><code>nes_vs_rgb</code> compiled but not exported</td><td>A0</td></tr><tr><td><code>cpu-no-pagecross</code></td><td>LDA absolute,X adds X without the indexed-read schedule (no page-cross cycle, no dummy read)</td><td>A1 (instruction timing)</td></tr><tr><td><code>pad-stuck</code></td><td>every controller read returns 1 (the core's declared fault switch)</td><td>A1 (<code>read_joy3</code>, the controller read during a DMC fetch)</td></tr><tr><td><code>a12-unfiltered</code></td><td><code>A12_FILTER_DOTS</code> = 0: every A12 rise clocks the scanline counter</td><td>A1 (the MMC3 rows)</td></tr><tr><td><code>mmc1-four-writes</code></td><td>MMC1's serial port commits on the fourth write instead of the fifth</td><td>A2 (mapper 1), A3 (the sample's MMC1 titles)</td></tr><tr><td><code>fds-byte-too-fast</code></td><td><code>FDS_CYCLES_PER_BYTE</code> = 49: a byte every 50 CPU cycles instead of 150</td><td>A4</td></tr><tr><td><code>noise-half-rate</code></td><td><code>NES_APU_NOISE_FULL_RATE</code> = 0: the noise channel an octave low; every board audio row still passes this</td><td>A5</td></tr><tr><td><code>zapper-blind</code></td><td>the Zapper's light sense always reads dark</td><td>A6</td></tr><tr><td><code>vs-colours-swapped</code></td><td>the VS colour entries' red and blue read in the wrong order</td><td>A7</td></tr><tr><td><code>state-outside-context</code></td><td>the noise channel's shift register kept in a file-scope variable</td><td>A8</td></tr><tr><td><code>hang-forever</code></td><td>MMC1's committing write enters a loop that never ends: the build never returns from a call</td><td>A2, with the mapper 1 item <code>TIMED OUT</code> at a 60 s deadline and the mapper 0, 2 and 3 items after it still scored (3 of 4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Faults some sections cannot see.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>FDS_READY_POLARITY</code> = 0 leaves every sampled disk drawing. This is why A4 requires the disk's program to be running (1.1, A4 step 5).</li><li><code>pad-stuck</code> leaves A6 green: the peripheral rows read the Zapper, the Vaus, the mat, the extra players and the microphone, none of which goes through the stuck pad bit. A6's own broken build is <code>zapper-blind</code>.</li><li>A0's isolation check (two machines in one instance, stepped alternately, picture and sound compared with solo runs) does <b>not</b> see <code>state-outside-context</code>: a shared noise register leaves both machines' audio identical to their solo runs over 240 frames. A8 sees it. A0's isolation check is a cheap first look, not the test of the save-state contract.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Worked example: the deadline, proved both ways.</b> <code>hang-forever</code> declares its <code>TIMED OUT</code> line as <code>EXPECT_TEXT</code>, because a red that was not a timeout would prove nothing about the deadline.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>run</th><th>same build (<code>hang-forever</code> alone), same rows (<code>--only A2 --rows \"m0|m1|m2|m3\"</code>)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>suite without the deadline</td><td>prints the A2 heading and nothing more; <b>no verdict</b> (stopped by an outside 300 s limit, exit 124)</td></tr><tr><td>suite with the deadline, <code>--deadline 60</code></td><td>the mapper 1 item <code>TIMED OUT</code>; <b>3 of 4</b> scored; <code>ACCEPTANCE RED</code>; exit 1; 66 s</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Result</b> (measured): the unpatched control is byte-identical to <code>build/nes.wasm</code>; the reference is green on every broken build's subset; <b>11 of 11 broken builds go red where they must</b>, <code>hang-forever</code> with its <code>TIMED OUT</code> line (<code>node test/acceptance/prove_can_fail.mjs</code>, exit 0, 337 s). The reference's full run under the deadline: <code>ACCEPTANCE GREEN, 9 of 9</code>, slowest item 15.3 s (<code>other/test28.nes</code>) against 600 s, 0 timeouts, <code>vs_play</code> 1,262 s against 7,200 s.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-80-acceptance-s4\">4. Glossary</h4>\n<p>Terms of this chapter and <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a>, each used only in this sense.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>term</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>frame</b></td><td>one whole picture period of the emulated machine, counted from power-on (the load of the cartridge or disk); \"after frame N\" means after the Nth frame has finished</td></tr><tr><td><b>item</b></td><td>the unit the suite measures under one deadline: a board row, a cartridge, a disk, an audio item, a peripheral row, a VS title, a save-state item, or A0's checks taken together</td></tr><tr><td><b>row</b></td><td>one entry of the board's row list (<code>test/BOARD-ROWS.json</code>): one test cartridge or test disk and how it is seated</td></tr><tr><td><b>tier 1</b></td><td>the rows a test program judges by its own verdict; A1 runs only these</td></tr><tr><td><b>reference</b></td><td>the build whose readings are recorded in <code>test/acceptance/expected.json</code>, named there by SHA-256</td></tr><tr><td><b>draws</b></td><td>for a cartridge (A2, A3): after 240 frames the CPU is not jammed and two or more palette indices are on screen</td></tr><tr><td><b>loads</b></td><td>for a disk (A4): draws, AND at least one of the last 60 frame-end program-counter samples is below <code>$E000</code></td></tr><tr><td><b>floor</b></td><td>the least number of A3 cartridges that must draw (193) or A4 disks that must load (41)</td></tr><tr><td><b>subset run</b></td><td>a run with <code>--rows</code> or <code>--max</code>; it says so on every summary line and is never an acceptance verdict</td></tr><tr><td><b>broken build</b></td><td>a copy of the reference core with ONE fault applied, used only to show a section can fail</td></tr><tr><td><b>PROOF row / CONTROL row</b></td><td>an A6 row whose predicate must read TRUE (PROOF) or FALSE (CONTROL)</td></tr><tr><td><b>COULD NOT RUN</b></td><td>a section that cannot load its full input set: exit 3, the missing input named, nothing scored</td></tr><tr><td><b>CANNOT-SCORE</b></td><td>a single row that gave no verdict; it is not a PASS</td></tr><tr><td><b>corpus</b></td><td>one pinned set of test cartridges (<a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a> section 1)</td></tr><tr><td><b>library</b></td><td>the user's own cartridge and disk files under the library root (<a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a> section 2)</td></tr><tr><td><b>verified dump</b></td><td>a library file whose name carries <code>[!]</code> (<a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a> section 2, the dump-marker rule)</td></tr><tr><td><b>drive firmware</b></td><td>the Disk System's own program image, supplied by the user (<a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus\">85-CORPUS.md</a> section 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>\n<section class=\"sect nesemu-chapter\" id=\"nesemu-85-corpus-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"nesemu-85-corpus\">The corpus: where the acceptance suite's inputs come from</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus-s1\">1. The three test-cartridge corpora (the board, A1; part of A2 and A5)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus-s2\">2. The cartridge library (A2, A3, A5, A6, A7, A8)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus-s3\">3. The Disk System inputs (A1's two disks, A4, part of A5 and A8)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-85-corpus-s4\">4. The reference's readings</a></nav>\n<p>The acceptance suite (<a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance\">80-ACCEPTANCE.md</a>) runs your build over four kinds of input: the test-cartridge corpora (section 1), the cartridge library (section 2), the Disk System inputs (section 3) and the reference's readings (section 4). Terms are defined in <a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance\">80-ACCEPTANCE.md</a> section 4.</p>\n<p><b>Rules: what the repository holds, and a missing input.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>None of the inputs is in the repository, and none ever will be.</b> What the repository holds is the <b>way to rebuild each input and the hash to check it by</b>.</li><li>A section that cannot load its full input set reports <b>COULD NOT RUN</b>, names what is missing, and exits 3; it never scores the part it found.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The test cartridges are other people's work, and commercial cartridge firmware, disk images and drive firmware are the user's own, local and never committed.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-85-corpus-s1\">1. The three test-cartridge corpora (the board, A1; part of A2 and A5)</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>corpus</th><th>source</th><th>pinned at (commit)</th><th>what it gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the blargg / community test suites</td><td><code>https://github.com/christopherpow/nes-test-roms</code></td><td><code>95d8f621ae55cee0d09b91519a8989ae0e64753b</code></td><td>265 prebuilt cartridges and two Disk System test disks</td></tr><tr><td>Holy Mapperel (the mapper-chip suite)</td><td><code>https://github.com/pinobatch/holy-mapperel</code></td><td><code>4e48b59432b5f1d3c011ac830e40ddf7ed5bbc61</code></td><td>source only: 35 cartridges are BUILT from it</td></tr><tr><td>AccuracyCoin</td><td><code>https://github.com/100thCoin/AccuracyCoin</code> (MIT)</td><td><code>673ef550db296136d52229961e7d39366116882a</code></td><td>one prebuilt cartridge, <code>AccuracyCoin.nes</code> (40,976 bytes), and its source <code>AccuracyCoin.asm</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>How to rebuild</b> (steps per corpus):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>The community suites.</b> Clone the repository and check out its pinned commit into <code>test/vendor/</code>, in a folder named after the repository. An identity probe checks one file: <code>other/nestest.nes</code>, 24,592 bytes, SHA-256 <code>f67d55fd6b3cf0bad1cc85f1df0d739c65b53e79cecb7fea8f77ec0eadab0004</code>.</li><li><b>Holy Mapperel.</b> Clone it at its pinned commit into <code>test/vendor/holy-mapperel</code> and build it, with Python 3 and Pillow, cc65 (ca65 and ld65 2.14 or later) and GNU Make:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>make mapperel-primary.nes</code>;</li><li>then run the repository's own build script (in its <code>tools/</code> folder), which writes the 35 cartridges into its test-cartridge folder.</li></ol>\n<p>The release tag v0.02 builds 31 cartridges and needs Python 3.9; the pinned commit is the one after the Python fix.</p></li><li><b>AccuracyCoin.</b> Clone it at its pinned commit into <code>test/vendor/accuracycoin</code>. Both files are checked by SHA-256:\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>file</th><th>SHA-256</th><th>taken over</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>AccuracyCoin.nes</code></td><td><code>4fe8c2bc9abc6f4d418da47b73f62cba89fcacd950fae763097a1681d650e839</code></td><td>the bytes as stored</td></tr><tr><td><code>AccuracyCoin.asm</code></td><td><code>497028b52945d6c3a64a37171bb5155dbd357d20dbb00709cad565b1c7264795</code></td><td>the text with LF line endings (the repository normalises text, so a CRLF checkout differs byte for byte)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The source is an input, not documentation: A1 reads the cartridge's test list and its counters' addresses from it. On this pinned copy it declares 144 scored tests; how A1 reads the cartridge is <a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance\">80-ACCEPTANCE.md</a> section 1.1, \"the five rows with their own protocol\".</p></li><li><b>Checking.</b> <code>node tools/fetch_tests.mjs</code> fetches what is missing and verifies; <code>node tools/fetch_tests.mjs --verify</code> only verifies. A missing pinned file is COULD NOT RUN (exit 3); a pinned file whose hash differs is a mismatch (exit 1).</li><li><b>Another location.</b> Each corpus root can be overridden by an environment variable: <code>NES_CORPUS_</code> followed by the corpus directory's name in capitals, each non-alphanumeric character written as <code>_</code>. Example: the mapper suite's directory <code>holy-mapperel</code> gives <code>NES_CORPUS_HOLY_MAPPEREL</code>.</li></ol>\n<p><b>The golden log</b> for nestest's automated mode is <code>test/golden/nestest.log</code>: 8,991 lines, 868,158 bytes (CRLF line endings), SHA-256 <code>627c8e180b1a924dfa705c5dc6958fad7ab75a62de556173caf880ccc1337540</code>, from <code>https://www.qmtpro.com/~nes/misc/nestest.log</code>; a byte-identical copy ships inside the first corpus as <code>other/nestest.log</code>. A1 compares every instruction's program counter, A, X, Y, P, S and cycle count with it.</p>\n<p><b>Which rows, and how each is seated</b>, is <code>test/BOARD-ROWS.json</code>; A1 runs the rows marked tier 1 (those a test program judges by its own verdict). A row may carry these fields:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>field</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>region</code></td><td>PAL (the container cannot say)</td></tr><tr><td><code>board_submapper</code></td><td>the chip revision the row is named for: the MMC6 row on mapper 4 submapper 1, the MMC3A row on mapper 4 submapper 4</td></tr><tr><td><code>expect_crc</code></td><td>the checksums a row that prints a checksum may print</td></tr><tr><td><code>requires_reset</code></td><td>the cartridge asks for a reset by writing <code>$81</code> to its status byte</td></tr><tr><td><code>drive_buttons</code></td><td>the prompted-button row</td></tr><tr><td><code>medium: \"fds\"</code>, with a frame budget</td><td>the two self-reporting test disks</td></tr><tr><td><code>reader: \"accuracycoin\"</code></td><td>the AccuracyCoin row, read by its own tally (<a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance\">80-ACCEPTANCE.md</a> section 1.1)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-85-corpus-s2\">2. The cartridge library (A2, A3, A5, A6, A7, A8)</h4>\n<p><b>Rules: the library.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The library is <b>user-supplied and strictly local</b>: iNES and UNIF files in one folder, <code>NES Games</code>, under a library root.</li><li><b>Point the suite at it with the environment variable <code>NES_LIBRARY</code></b>: the root, which holds <code>NES Games</code> and <code>FDS</code>.</li><li>Every file the suite uses is named in <code>test/acceptance/expected.json</code> or <code>test/acceptance/sets.mjs</code> by its path <b>relative to that root</b>, and <code>expected.json</code> records its SHA-256. A library on another machine either holds the same bytes or the section names the file that is absent.</li></ol>\n<p><b>The dump-marker rule</b> (which copies may be used at all), as a decision table on the file name; the first matching line decides:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>the file name carries</th><th>the copy is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>[!]</code></td><td>a verified dump, preferred</td></tr><tr><td><code>[b]</code>, <code>[o]</code>, <code>[h]</code>, <code>[t]</code>, <code>[T±…]</code>, <code>[a]</code>, <code>[p]</code> or <code>[f]</code></td><td>rejected</td></tr><tr><td>a round-bracket tag naming a hack, prototype, beta, demo, sample, homebrew or pirate build (the words matched, in any case: Hack, Trainer, Pirate, Unl, Prototype, Proto, Beta, Demo, Sample, Aftermarket, Homebrew)</td><td>rejected</td></tr><tr><td>none of the above</td><td>unverified, usable</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>How the sets were chosen</b>: by rules, so they can be re-made (<code>test/acceptance/record.mjs</code> applies them). \"Path order\" is the order of the paths relative to the library root.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>section</th><th>rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A2 mappers</td><td>for every mapper number the specification documents, try candidates in this order and take the first that the reference mounts <b>as that number</b> (after the host's header correction) and that <b>draws</b> (two or more palette indices on screen at frame 240, CPU not jammed): (1) the library's files the host will mount as that number (a <code>.nes</code> file is filed under its header's number, corrected by the host's header-correction table, except when the header's number is 6 or 17 or the table's row sets only the region; a UNIF file under the number its board resolves to), sorted verified <code>.nes</code> files first, then verified UNIF, then unverified <code>.nes</code>, then unverified UNIF, each group in path order, at most twelve candidates in all; then (2) the <code>.nes</code> files of the two test corpora (the mapper suite first, then the community suites) whose header carries that number</td></tr><tr><td>A3 library sample</td><td>every 10th verified-dump <code>.nes</code> file of <code>NES Games</code> in path order, starting with the first (positions 1, 11, 21, ...)</td></tr><tr><td>A4 Disk System</td><td>every 9th disk image of <code>FDS/Games</code> and <code>FDS/GamesPak2</code> together, in path order, starting with the first (positions 1, 10, 19, ...)</td></tr><tr><td>A5, A6, A7, A8</td><td>named one by one in <code>test/acceptance/sets.mjs</code>, each with its reason</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-85-corpus-s3\">3. The Disk System inputs (A1's two disks, A4, part of A5 and A8)</h4>\n<p>The drive firmware is <b>user-supplied, loaded from local disk, never committed, never shipped</b>. The suite uses one image and checks its hash:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>file, under the library root</th><th>bytes</th><th>SHA-256</th><th>role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>FDS/[BIOS] Family Computer Disk System (Japan) (Rev 1).bin</code></td><td>8,192</td><td><code>99c18490ed9002d9c6d999b9d8d15be5c051bdfa7cc7e73318053c9a994b0178</code></td><td>the drive firmware every Disk System run uses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The disk images are in two folders, <code>FDS/Games</code> and <code>FDS/GamesPak2</code>. A disk image may carry the 16-byte <code>FDS\\x1A</code> container header, which the host strips; what remains must be a whole number of 65,500-byte sides (<a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds\">50-FDS.md</a>).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-85-corpus-s4\">4. The reference's readings</h4>\n<p><code>test/acceptance/expected.json</code> holds what the <b>reference build</b> read on every input: board verdicts, the chosen files and their hashes, draw results, frame hashes, audio <b>features</b> (band energies per 1/15 s window, never samples), probe values, VS colour-table hashes. <b>No byte of any cartridge, disk or firmware is in it.</b> It is written by <code>node test/acceptance/record.mjs</code>, which refuses any build but the reference, <code>build/nes.wasm</code>, built from <code>src/</code> by <code>tools/build.sh</code>; the build it recorded is named in the file by SHA-256 (<a href=\"#nesemu-80-acceptance\">80-ACCEPTANCE.md</a> section 2 gives it).</p></section>\n<section class=\"sect nesemu-chapter\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit\">The fix audit: bugs other projects published, checked against this core</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s1\">1. What was read</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s2\">2. How each finding was judged</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s3\">3. The result</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s4\">4. The defects, ranked</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s5\">5. Our own failure list, checked</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s6\">6. The Disk System error screens</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s7\">7. The comparison tables</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s8\">8. The bug hunt: every finding taken to a status, and fixed where it can be</a></nav>\n<p>This audit was made on 7 October 2026. The status given for each finding below is what this core does now.</p>\n<p>Every NES emulator that has been worked on for years has a public record of what it got wrong and fixed: release notes, changelogs, commit messages, issue trackers. The community keeps lists too: the NESdev wiki's tricky games, real-hardware game bugs and emulator tests, the forum's threads, and the TAS community's game notes. This chapter reads all of that record and asks one question of every entry: <b>does this core have the same bug?</b> The answer is measured, not assumed.</p>\n<p>The record is used here only as a list of places to look. Where this chapter says the core is right or wrong, the evidence is our own: a test cartridge's own verdict, a run of the named game, or a reading of our own code. Other emulators are named only in the two comparison tables in §7, never as the authority for how the console behaves.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s1\">1. What was read</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Five NES emulators' complete development histories</b>, every core commit title, with the change itself read wherever the title did not say what was wrong. Also one multi-system emulator's NES core history (1,580 commits).</li><li><b>Their issue trackers, release notes and changelogs</b>, all versions.</li><li><b>The NESdev wiki:</b> tricky-to-emulate games, real-hardware game bugs, the emulator test list, every mapper page, and the Disk System pages.</li><li><b>The NESdev forum:</b> about 1,900 threads screened.</li><li><b>TASVideos</b> game resources and accuracy pages; speedrun notes; Reddit as leads only.</li></ul>\n<p>That is 2,221 source-register rows covering about 57,800 items reviewed. They produced <b>2,723 findings</b>, each naming a game, a test cartridge or a board, and a behaviour.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s2\">2. How each finding was judged</h4>\n<p>Each finding gets exactly one verdict:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>verdict</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WE HAVE IT</td><td>reproduced on this core, or our code implements the wrong mechanism (quoted by file and line)</td></tr><tr><td>WE DON'T</td><td>this core behaves correctly; the evidence is named</td></tr><tr><td>ALREADY FIXED BY US</td><td>this core had it and it was fixed before the audit</td></tr><tr><td>UNTESTABLE</td><td>no cartridge, no way to reach the scene, or a board this core does not implement</td></tr><tr><td>NOT APPLICABLE</td><td>a frontend item, a change internal to another program, or no checkable claim</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The evidence, strongest first: a test cartridge states its own verdict; agreement with a pinned reference emulator's output (agreement, never proof); our own observation of a run; a reading of our code. A proposed fix either follows documented or measured hardware behaviour, or is tuned: chosen to match an outcome.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s3\">3. The result</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>verdict</th><th>findings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WE HAVE IT</td><td>544</td></tr><tr><td>WE DON'T</td><td>1,450</td></tr><tr><td>ALREADY FIXED BY US</td><td>126</td></tr><tr><td>UNTESTABLE</td><td>437</td></tr><tr><td>NOT APPLICABLE</td><td>166</td></tr><tr><td>all</td><td>2,723</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The 544 WE HAVE IT rows share about 110 mechanisms. At the time of the audit AccuracyCoin, a 144-test accuracy cartridge, gave <b>114 pass and 30 fail</b> on this core, every one of the 30 failures among those mechanisms. With the fixes below it reads <b>144 of 144</b>, and the cartridge's 20 October 2026 build, with 146 tests, reads <b>146 of 146</b>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s4\">4. The defects, ranked</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.1 Games on the unsupported list with no playable alternative</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>title</th><th>what the core lacked</th><th>now</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P1</td><td>Nintendo World Championships 1990</td><td>mapper 105: MMC1 with a bank lock released by $A000 bit 4 going 0 then 1, and a 30-bit M2 timer IRQ whose length is set by DIP switches</td><td>built (mapper 105)</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>Strike Wolf</td><td>mapper 36: an ASIC with an internal register R (read at $4100 bits 4-5, incremented or loaded by $4100-$4103 writes, copied to the PRG pins by a $8000 write), and an 8 KiB CHR bank at $4200; a light-gun game</td><td>built (mapper 36); its light-gun hits follow the gun model of <a href=\"#nesemu-60-peripherals\">60-PERIPHERALS.md</a> 3.2 and 3.2a: the program needs the grey backdrop its greyscale bit makes to read dark, which the one measured set contradicts</td></tr><tr><td>P3</td><td>Tui Do Woo Ma Jeung</td><td>its header names an obsolete mapper; the board is mapper 122 (two 4 KiB CHR banks chosen by even and odd addresses)</td><td>built (mapper 122 and a header-table override row; the board is the JY043, not the KS-7058)</td></tr><tr><td>P4</td><td>Street Heroes</td><td>mapper 262: MMC3 plus a $4100 register (a 256 KiB CHR bank per quarter, a CHR-RAM switch, a title jumper on read) and four-screen VRAM</td><td>built (mapper 262)</td></tr><tr><td>P5</td><td>Chuugoku Taitei</td><td>its header names mapper 116; the board is mapper 512: MMC3 plus a $4100 mode register, six nametable pages and always-on work RAM. The \"29,488 cycles per frame\" seen in capture is the jam on the wrong board, not a region.</td><td>built (mapper 512)</td></tr><tr><td>P6</td><td>Study Box</td><td>mapper 186 plus cassette emulation</td><td>built: the cartridge (<a href=\"#nesemu-44-mappers-tail\">44-MAPPERS-TAIL.md</a> Mapper 186) boots to its own no-tape screen, and the tape deck plays <code>.studybox</code> tape images: Enjoy English 01 loads its lessons from tape (twelve tapes checked)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.2 Defects a player sees</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>defect</th><th>files</th><th>basis</th><th>now</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>R1</td><td>VRC7 register select decodes address line A4 only; Tiny Toon Adventures 2 (J) uses A3 and jams</td><td>2</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R2</td><td>The browser plays PAL and Dendy games at the NTSC audio rate: PAL runs 7.65% fast and 1.3 semitones sharp with sound on</td><td>591</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R3</td><td>The Family BASIC keyboard is never attached by the site, so Family BASIC and Playbox BASIC stay blank</td><td>4</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed: the host attaches the keyboard for these titles (<a href=\"#nesemu-60-peripherals\">60-PERIPHERALS.md</a> 3.8)</td></tr><tr><td>R4</td><td>Fresh battery RAM starts at $00; Famicom Jump II reads a vector from it and jams</td><td>6</td><td>tuned</td><td>fixed: a fresh save starts at <code>$00</code> unless the fresh-save table gives the cartridge its own byte</td></tr><tr><td>R5</td><td>The zero power-on fill leaves Minna no Taabou blank; it needs $0011 non-zero</td><td>6</td><td>tuned</td><td>fixed 2026-10-23: the power-on state is a measured console's (front-loader RAM pattern, measured palette), under which Minna no Taabou draws (<a href=\"#nesemu-00-machine\">00-MACHINE.md</a> 6.5)</td></tr><tr><td>R6</td><td>VRC6 $B003 mirroring bits are never applied</td><td>25</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R7</td><td>MMC1 SOROM/SXROM/SZROM work-RAM banking is missing</td><td>56</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R8</td><td>Namco 175/340 nametable control (Family Circuit '91)</td><td>16</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R9</td><td>The Acclaim MC-ACC IRQ counter runs as a plain MMC3 counter</td><td>13</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R10</td><td>$2004 reads during rendering return sprite memory, not the evaluation bus (Micro Machines)</td><td>5+</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed (<a href=\"#nesemu-20-ppu\">20-PPU.md</a> 5.1, 9.4, 9.5)</td></tr><tr><td>R11</td><td>Every old-format cartridge gets work RAM at $6000, even boards that have none</td><td>many</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed (the VRC2 $6000 latch modelled, the Bandai EEPROM boards excepted)</td></tr><tr><td>R12</td><td>The header-correction table never corrects mirroring</td><td>several</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R13</td><td>Files shorter than their header are refused; 20 are good dumps</td><td>20</td><td>tuned</td><td>fixed: such files load</td></tr><tr><td>R14</td><td>The mapper 15 mode table differs from the documented board</td><td>53</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed 2026-10-23: the documented table is on; the one file it stops is a five-byte conversion hack of a mapper-166 program (<a href=\"#nesemu-43-mappers-unlicensed\">43-MAPPERS-UNLICENSED.md</a>, mapper 15)</td></tr><tr><td>R15</td><td>Mixed CHR RAM and CHR firmware is not implemented for TQROM and the Waixing boards</td><td>69</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R16</td><td>The RAMBO-1 IRQ delay is not implemented</td><td>22</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R17</td><td>The MMC5 vertical split is not implemented</td><td>2</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed (a 21-pixel difference remains on scanline 0 of SDF, a picture-unit matter)</td></tr><tr><td>R18</td><td>Turning rendering off mid-line does not corrupt sprite memory as it should</td><td>few</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R19</td><td>PAL and Dendy red/green emphasis bits are not swapped</td><td>PAL titles</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R20</td><td>The PAL picture chip's sprite-memory refresh is missing</td><td>PAL titles</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R21</td><td>Expansion-sound register details (Namco 163 address wrap, Sunsoft 5B envelope, VRC7 write timing, MMC5/VRC6 phase)</td><td>few</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed; the Namco 163 levels changed for submappers 1/2 and not for 3/4; the VRC6 output inversion is on (default 1); the Sunsoft 5B amplifier curve is built but off (it moves nothing)</td></tr><tr><td>R22</td><td>Disk System sound: envelope timing, modulation-table pointer, wave reads, output filter</td><td>all disks with modulation</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R23</td><td>Disk System status registers ($4030 bits 3 and 7, $4023 gating, $4033 bit 7, refresh watchdog)</td><td>few</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed, including the $4030 re-read</td></tr><tr><td>R24</td><td>Bus conflicts are never emulated</td><td>few</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R25</td><td>VRC2 is treated as VRC4</td><td>few</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R26</td><td>The Bandai serial EEPROM is not implemented, so those saves are lost</td><td>few</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R27</td><td>Some bank latches are masked too narrowly</td><td>few</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed</td></tr><tr><td>R28</td><td>PAL sample-channel DMA follows the NTSC halt rule; the PAL black border is missing</td><td>PAL titles</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>fixed, both halves</td></tr><tr><td>R29-R31</td><td>Homebrew that is blank, jams or differs here but runs on the reference</td><td>18</td><td>investigated</td><td>no core defect (below)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>R29-R31, investigated, no core defect.</b> R29 paints from power-on palette memory (the measured palette since 2026-10-23: AIR Demo 06-10 and Junk Demo draw; GITS stays blank). R30: Jet Fighter Z jams on the reference too, at the same cycle; the MMC5 demos run picture-unit registers as code during rendering and part on an unmeasured detail. R31: two identical on the pixels, one loaded without the header table, one a PAL power-on line difference, one a <code>$2007</code> write during rendering (T11), one power-on palette.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.3 Defects only a test cartridge sees</h5>\n<p>These are twenty-two clusters, each following a hardware rule: the SH* unofficial stores under DMA, interrupt polling in branches, the CPU's internal and external data latches, the sample-channel DMA's halt-cycle reads, abort and reload timing, DMA register gating, double OAM DMA, the frame-IRQ flag timing, the controller strobe phase, several picture-chip timing details, power-on noise period, and header edge cases. Each is listed by finding number in the full audit.</p>\n<p><b>CPU and DMA, T1-T9, now:</b> T1 fixed (SH* stores under DMA); T2 fixed (branch polls ORed); T3 fixed (internal and external data latches); T4 fixed for the picture-unit registers and <code>$4015</code> (the Disk System's <code>$4030</code> and cartridge registers left open); T5 load timing fixed, explicit and implicit aborts fixed (their windows mapped from a per-cycle reference trace); T6 fixed (DMA register activation); T7 fixed (<code>INC $4014</code>); T8 fixed (frame-interrupt flag phase); T9 fixed (OUT pins on the get-to-put transition).</p>\n<p><b>Picture unit, T10-T14, now:</b> T10 fixed: <code>$2002</code> sprite-flag timing, sprite output units, no evaluation on the pre-render line, background serial-in and reload dot, the secondary-OAM address register, the read buffer's fetch slot and ALE + read, and the hybrid nametable read (on by default, with fine Y from the data dot). T11 fixed 2026-10-23: the <code>$2007</code>-write-during-rendering rule is on; the demos it blanked paint from the power-on palette, now a measured one. T12 fixed 2026-10-23: the <code>$2006</code> AND rule is on, with the PAL access phase set so that no comparison row is lost. T13 not changed: no test here decides the write window. T14: A12 driven when rendering stops and the undriven pattern read fixed; the <code>$2003</code> row copy and OAM decay not changed (not established on hardware); the <code>$2004</code> write <code>&amp; $FC</code> fixed.</p>\n<p><b>Also fixed:</b> the power-on noise period; the sample level and triangle step at reset; the Vs. System's missing short-noise mode; the Arkanoid controller's ninth bit; the NES 2.0 exponent size form; the MMC1A fixed-bank bypass; SNROM's work-RAM enable line; MMC5's sample read mode and its interrupt. Not changed: the MMC3 consecutive-reload corruption (not established on hardware), per-title MMC3 revisions and two container tolerances (they would be tuned), power-of-two bank masking (the value past the data is not established).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s5\">5. Our own failure list, checked</h4>\n<p>At the time of the audit the site's unsupported list named 156 cartridges. Each one that loads was run on this core, and the ones that state no verdict were also run against the reference.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>outcome</th><th>entries</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>test cartridges that print PASSED and then hold still; listed as hangs in error</td><td>42</td></tr><tr><td>draw nothing, or a static picture, exactly as the reference does</td><td>49</td></tr><tr><td>board not implemented (including P1-P6)</td><td>37</td></tr><tr><td>defects in this core (R1, R3, R4, R5)</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>differ from the reference, cause not yet established (R29-R31)</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>bad dumps</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>not applicable (VS DualSystem titles, a revision-A-only test, a missing file)</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s6\">6. The Disk System error screens</h4>\n<p>Fifteen disk images never hand over to their own program; most show the drive's \"DISK TROUBLE ERR.20\". Twelve of them have <b>no boot file at all</b>. They are second disks and data disks (later Fruits Mahjong volumes, Disk 2 sides, the Kouhen halves of two-part games), which need their program disk inserted first. With nothing loaded, the drive firmware's licence check fails, and that is the error shown. The reference shows the same screen on every disk checked. This is correct hardware behaviour, not a core fault. The fix is to label those disks in the library.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s7\">7. The comparison tables</h4>\n<p>The two tables below are the only place on this tab where other emulators are named. They record which program's history each finding came from, and how those findings fared against this core. They are a comparison, not a reference.</p>\n<div class=nesemu-comparison data-declared=90-FIX-HISTORY-AUDIT/rival-findings><div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>where the finding came from</th><th>findings</th><th>WE HAVE IT</th><th>WE DON'T</th><th>ALREADY FIXED BY US</th><th>UNTESTABLE</th><th>NOT APPLICABLE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NESdev forum</td><td>1,182</td><td>203</td><td>639</td><td>60</td><td>186</td><td>94</td></tr><tr><td>NESdev wiki</td><td>410</td><td>81</td><td>222</td><td>12</td><td>82</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Mesen (version 1) history</td><td>208</td><td>40</td><td>116</td><td>15</td><td>26</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>puNES history</td><td>199</td><td>43</td><td>90</td><td>4</td><td>47</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>BizHawk NESHawk history</td><td>193</td><td>46</td><td>116</td><td>14</td><td>14</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Nestopia / Nestopia UE history</td><td>149</td><td>35</td><td>86</td><td>11</td><td>13</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>FCEUX history</td><td>133</td><td>23</td><td>81</td><td>3</td><td>20</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Mesen2 / MesenCE history</td><td>102</td><td>40</td><td>37</td><td>2</td><td>15</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>TASVideos</td><td>97</td><td>19</td><td>33</td><td>2</td><td>32</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>other community sources</td><td>50</td><td>14</td><td>30</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><!--/declared-comparison-->\n<div class=nesemu-comparison data-declared=90-FIX-HISTORY-AUDIT/source-register><div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>source</th><th>what was read</th><th>items reviewed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mesen (version 1), Mesen2 and MesenCE</td><td>Mesen 1: full core commit history (1,677), 854 issues and pull requests, 17 releases. Mesen2/MesenCE: full NES commit history (614), 379 pull requests, releases, the 2.2.0 changelog</td><td>3,547</td></tr><tr><td>puNES</td><td>full core history, every changelog version 0.11-0.112, issue tracker</td><td>2,781</td></tr><tr><td>Nestopia, Nestopia UE and FCEUX</td><td>full changelogs, core histories, issue trackers</td><td>5,695</td></tr><tr><td>BizHawk NESHawk</td><td>full NES core history (1,580 commits), issues</td><td>3,928</td></tr><tr><td>NESdev wiki</td><td>tricky games, game bugs, emulator tests, every mapper page, Disk System pages</td><td>355</td></tr><tr><td>NESdev forum</td><td>about 1,900 threads screened</td><td>36,564</td></tr><tr><td>TASVideos, speedrun notes, Reddit (leads only)</td><td>game resources, accuracy tests</td><td>3,255</td></tr><tr><td>the follow-up reading</td><td>the 2.2.0 changelog again, the failure-list titles, the emulator test list</td><td>1,640</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><!--/declared-comparison-->\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-90-fix-history-audit-s8\">8. The bug hunt: every finding taken to a status, and fixed where it can be</h4>\n<p>From 29 October 2026 every finding this audit judged WE HAVE IT was taken to a status on the core the site serves, and the ones that can be fixed are being fixed in turn, the games a player sees first. A fix follows the hardware: no game is patched, and a change that costs any other title is reworked until it costs nothing, or set aside with its evidence and the condition that would reopen it.</p>\n<p><b>The status of all 544 findings, 29 October 2026, at the close of the hunt:</b></p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>status</th><th>findings</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>fixed</td><td>469</td><td>the core follows the hardware rule (424 before the bug hunt began, 45 during it)</td></tr><tr><td>open, can be fixed</td><td>3</td><td>built behind switches that stay off until a decision: bus conflicts on GxROM boards (no header marks which boards have them, so it needs a list of files), and the front-loading NES's controller double-read (nothing in a file says whether the game ran on an NES or a Famicom)</td></tr><tr><td>open, no reference</td><td>23</td><td>nothing establishes what the hardware does (no measurement, no test cartridge with an answer), so any value would be a guess; each has the condition that would reopen it</td></tr><tr><td>set aside</td><td>38</td><td>measured and set aside with evidence: the dot-257 write glitch (no test decides its timing), sprite-memory decay and the <code>$2003</code> corruption (they depend on temperature and alignment), the MMC3 revision per title, the multicart solder pads</td></tr><tr><td>not a fault</td><td>11</td><td>on re-measuring or re-reading, a dump or a cartridge's own behaviour, not this core (six of them the mid-frame controller timing, which did not reproduce)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The audit's section 3 items, item by item: every priority title with no playable alternative (P1-P6) and every player-visible defect (R1-R31) is fixed or shown not to be a fault of this core; of the test-cartridge clusters (T1-T22), T13 and parts of T14, T15, T18, T20 and T21 stay open for want of a reference, and T19 was not reproduced.</p>\n<p><b>Fixed on 29 October 2026:</b></p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Mapper 158, Tengen's Alien Syndrome board.</b> It is the RAMBO-1 with one wire moved: the chip's character address line A17 selects the nametable page, as on Nintendo's TLSROM. The three files had run on the MMC3 board their header names. They now run on their own board, with the RAMBO-1's counter. The picture agrees with the reference as before, at a one-frame lead.</li><li><b>Creatom (mapper 132).</b> Its protection chip answers reads at any $41xx address with A8 set, not only at $4100. With the narrow decode, falling blocks froze in mid-air when moved left, which the game does not do on a console. They now fall. The other four titles on this chip do not change.</li><li><b>Silva Saga.</b> A new cartridge showed three saved games at level 1, and loading one gave a broken map, because an all-zero save passes the game's own check. Its fresh save now starts at $FF, under which the three slots are empty, as with random contents. Ultima: Exodus, named with it in the audit, was checked and shows no such saves.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Fixed the same day, by chip:</b></p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>MMC5:</b> each graphics bank keeps the upper bits it was written with; writes to the chip's extra memory outside the picture do not land; its save memory is wired as the real boards' one or two chips; it listens only to the exact <code>$2001</code>; the MMC5A's timer is built.</li><li><b>Other mapper chips:</b> bus conflicts on BNROM and mapper 72; SEROM and SUROM banking; the VRC6's full nametable table; the Namco 163 using console memory as pattern data; the FME-7's four work-memory pages; work-memory enables on the VRC4 and the Sunsoft-4; Bandai's register ranges per board; mapper 83's paged memory; mapper 91's second board; and the CoolBoy/MindKids multicart board (mapper 268), so its large multicarts start.</li><li><b>VS. System picture parts:</b> they cannot read back sprite or palette memory, as measured on a cabinet's part. <b>Controllers:</b> a sample-channel fetch from an address the console decodes as the controller port no longer moves the buttons along.</li><li><b>Disk System:</b> the disk keeps turning while the drive writes.</li><li><b>Saves:</b> 95 files whose header leaves out the battery the cartridge has now keep their saves.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Checked and not a fault of this core (29 October 2026):</b> The King of Fighters 99 and the Chinese God Slayer show one colour on the reference too; The Jetsons (Japan) now matches the reference on every frame over 3,000 frames; seven pixels on one line of Don Doko Don 2 come from a measured picture-unit read the reference does not have; the few pixels that differ in Metal Slader Glory and the Street Fighter Zero 2 demo come from two timing details where the reference is the one that differs from the documentation.</p></section>";
function nesAcceptanceEmulationTabHTML(d) {
  return '<div class="gbemu nesemu nesemu-acceptance">' + NES_EMU_ACCEPTANCE_HTML + '</div>';
}
